    "blurb": "The acoustic portion shines with a sublime soundboard capture of It’s A Man's World and Friend Of The Devil. The electric half delivers an adventurous The Other One marathon and the off‑beat Cowboy Song* instrumental, though some numbers feel uneven. Overall the show balances high‑energy improvisation with solid songcraft.",
    "review": "April 9, 1970 at Fillmore West finds the Dead in a transitional groove: the early acoustic set is rendered crystal clear on the matrix recording, letting Pigpen’s organ and harmonica textures breathe through It’s A Man's World and Friend Of The Devil. After a brief drum interlude the band launches into an electric marathon that peaks with a sprawling The Other One—Jerry’s lead lines weaving seamlessly around Phil’s bass foundation and the dual‑drum thunder of Mickey and Bill. The quirky Cowboy Song* instrumental provides a surprising, off‑beat detour that many fans cite as a highlight, even if a few reviewers felt it dragged in places. While the electric set has its uneven moments, the overall energy is high, making this show a compelling snapshot of the Dead’s evolving improvisational language.",
